these woods on the highway to Minsk were  the site of a mass murder and right next  to the improvised memorial is a  restaurant by a gym by a team let's go  and eat is just a short drive away for  the residents of the Belarusian capital  zhenya Sorbonne Norwich thinks it's  horrible that people dine here the 29  year old is handing out flyers to the  guests he wants to ensure the atrocities  committed here are not forgotten and  that the restaurant is closed some of  the visitors are aware of where they're  going and some aren't and some people  don't mind eating on bones xeni stands  vigil outside the restaurant every  weekend he's a critic of the government  and sees himself as an opposition  activist he's the chairman of the young  front a nationalistic youth movement  pushing for Democratic Change the  activists call this the restaurant of  death and they stand outside it in  shifts the owner of the restaurant  doesn't want to speak to us and were not  allowed to film beyond the driveway the  activists say when the restaurant  complex was built the official border of  the mass grave was shifted but  ultimately no one knows where the  memorial should begin or end Joseph  Stalin had between 30,000 and 250,000  people shot here in the late 30s and  early 40s declared enemies of the state  those murdered were innocent victims of  the Soviet dictators purges it was  shortly before the end of the Soviet  Union that the knowledge of this site  became public since then it has become  an impromptu people's memorial and a  centre of opposition activity zhenya  Sorbonne Norwich came here the first  time when he was 15 with his father  usually it smells like pine needles and  leaves in the woods but here you have  the feeling that thousands of candles  are burning denise's great-granduncle is  one of the victims buried here he was a  teacher  Denis has sworn to defend the memorial I  just can't give this place off I don't  know how I could ever close my eyes -  cooter party I won't be able to sleep at  night if I do when I come here something  shifts in me I start thinking  differently a half a year ago the  Belarusian government set up an official  monument to the victims of Stalin's  secret police at the edge of the forest  the government removed dozens of large  crosses and built a fence around the  whole area officials said the crosses  had been put up illegally for zinnias  the move was yet another warning signal  he's concerned the government wants to  take control of the memorial and points  out the country's president Alexander  Lukashenko has often praised Stalin's  legacy we have to have a plan on how to  finish fixing up kora potty but no one  should be able to do anything here  without permission what's going on here  are demonstrations it must be in  someone's interest these are political  protests Lukashenko has also said that  the restaurant by the mass grave doesn't  bother him but in Minsk not everyone  agrees it depends where the restaurant  is it's not in the middle of the crosses  is it it's somewhere on the edge near  the exit I think that's fine in Minsk  everything is right and proper motion  there are bones everywhere in Belarus we  don't know everything that happened here  but life has to go on so now people go  to a spot where people were killed to  have a good time it's not right  Zenoss agrees he makes the bus journey  from Minsk to Kiribati at least twice a  week he has been protesting against the  restaurant here for a year and  demonstrators want to keep fighting  Zenoss and the others see this  restaurant as an attack on the memorial  and for them that means it's an attack  on the people of Belarus we don't really  have anything of our own anymore  but we do have kuta party in the US they  are here and we have to defend them if  the government destroy SCOOTER party I  won't call myself a Belarusian anymore  they sing for Belarus and for the  victims here in the woods it's a prayer  for the dead but zhen